---
import FeaturesLayout from '../../layouts/FeaturesLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<FeaturesLayout
  currentSlug="routing"
  title="Routing - Route tree & SPA navigation in compodocx"
  description="How compodocx documents your Angular routes as a typed tree, and how the static output navigates: SPA page swaps, hash deep links, sub-path hosting."
>
  <p class="text-cdx-text-secondary text-lg">
    Two kinds of routing meet here: the routes of your Angular app, which compodocx documents, and
    the navigation of the generated static site.
  </p>

  <h2>The routes page</h2>
  <p>
    compodocx reads your routes through the type checker and shows them on
    <code class="cdx-code">routes.html</code>, one indented tree per router and per unmounted route
    array. The page exists only when the project has routes. Turn it all off with
    <code class="cdx-code">--disableRoutes</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    filename="main.ts"
    code={`bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding())],
});`}
  />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Routers.</strong> Each <code class="cdx-code">provideRouter</code> call reached from
      <code class="cdx-code">bootstrapApplication</code> is one router. Named configs,
      <code class="cdx-code">mergeApplicationConfig</code>, and spreads are followed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Route arrays.</strong> Recognized by their <code class="cdx-code"
        >@angular/router</code
      > type, never by name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lazy routes.</strong>
      <code class="cdx-code">loadChildren</code> arrays expand below their route, and <code
        class="cdx-code">loadComponent</code
      > targets link to the component.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Unmounted routes.</strong> An exported route array no router mounts, such as a library shipping
      feature routes, gets a section of its own.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code class="cdx-code">RouterModule.forRoot</code>, <code class="cdx-code">forChild</code>, and
    string
    <code class="cdx-code">loadChildren</code> are not read.
  </p>

  <h2>Links back from your symbols</h2>
  <p>
    Component pages list the routes they render at, route arrays the places they are mounted at, and
    functional guards and resolvers the routes that use them, with the property (<code
      class="cdx-code">/account - canActivate</code
    >). Feature pages show the routes of their members as a tree, and the command palette finds
    routes by their full path.
  </p>

  <h2>Navigating the generated site</h2>
  <p>
    The output is static HTML with real page URLs. A small client script intercepts internal links,
    fetches the page, swaps the content area, and updates the URL with
    <code class="cdx-code">history.pushState</code>, so no framework runtime ships with your docs.
  </p>

  <h2>Tab activation by hash</h2>
  <p>
    Each entity tab has a stable hash id. Sharing a URL like <code class="cdx-code">#api</code> opens
    the API tab, a member anchor opens the tab holding it, and <code class="cdx-code">#L42</code> scrolls
    the Source tab to that line. Tab panels win over headings with the same id, and hash updates use
    <code class="cdx-code">history.replaceState</code> so the browser&rsquo;s history isn&rsquo;t polluted.
  </p>

  <h2>No first-paint flash</h2>
  <p>
    Hash-targeted content is hidden during initial parse via a
    <code class="cdx-code">cdx-hash-pending</code> class on
    <code class="cdx-code">&lt;html&gt;</code>; it is removed after the initial hash is applied, so
    deep-linked pages never flash their default state before settling.
  </p>

  <h2>Hosting anywhere</h2>
  <p>
    Links between pages are relative, so the docs can sit at a domain root, in a sub-folder, or in a
    per-version folder under <code class="cdx-code">/documentation/&lt;label&gt;/</code> with no base-path
    setting. A site opened from disk keeps its interactive parts too; only search needs a server, because
    Pagefind fetches its index.
  </p>
  <p>
    The version switcher follows <code class="cdx-code">redirects.json</code>, written with every
    version, so a hop lands on the same symbol when its page moved between releases.
  </p>
</FeaturesLayout>
